A component abstraction should remain **ordinary Moth data**.
Its author chooses its public type and const functions. A page decides where
that data becomes output.

### A deliberately small result type

This is an illustrative source-backed `@html` design, not a committed
constructor or public signature:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    HtmlPart = |
        body String = "",
        stylesheets String = "",
    |

    html_page #|parts {HtmlPart}| -> HtmlPart:
        body ~= ""
        stylesheets ~= ""

        loop parts |part|:
            ~body = [body, part.body]
            ~stylesheets = [stylesheets, part.stylesheets]
        ;

        return HtmlPart(
            body = body,
            stylesheets = stylesheets
        )
    ;
]

A template is the normal way to concatenate Strings in Moth. The composite
result contains HTML and a structural String of stylesheet references. There
is no hidden page registration, renderer instance or component lifecycle.

A real library will also need a way to carry independent `head`
contributions. Their representation remains an API decision: concatenating
head snippets into one String loses the boundaries needed for the HTML
builder to deduplicate them individually. Do not assume the `HtmlPart` shape
above is complete.

### Keep local slots local

The Basic example's `card` owns its named `title` slot. An imported
or reused card cannot reach an enclosing page's builder slots. That
parent-only routing rule keeps deep composition predictable and prevents
an intermediate component from silently changing where inserts go.

Const functions can compose several finished `HtmlPart` values across
file and module boundaries. They evaluate under the compiler's constant
rules, never call runtime DOM functions and cannot invent new declarations
or source code.

### Carry resources, not generated URLs

A component's `stylesheets` field can be formed from file values such as
`@sidebar.css`. The String retains resource origins through composition.
The builder later resolves URLs for the destination document and deduplicates
stylesheets by stable origin, preserving first use order.

There is a remaining builder contract to implement: today's
`stylesheets` slot accepts exactly **one** CSS resource per direct insert.
The composed multi-resource String used on the Basic page is proposed final
behaviour, not accepted by the current slot validator. It must retain each
CSS origin and refuse stray text, site-root pieces and non-CSS resources.

Generic helpers should not compare structural Strings to guess whether they
refer to the same resource. A URL is output-context dependent. CSS rules that
all instances share belong in a separate resource. Inline `$css` suits
small values specific to one instance, such as a CSS custom property.

### Types and boundaries keep reuse safe

A nominal `HtmlPart` is a useful library contract, not an automatic
conversion from an arbitrary record. Const construction checks every field
and every called function. Reusable source packages publish only their
selected declarations through `$export`.

Runtime components and browser handles still obey Moth's shared/exclusive
access rules and mandatory lifetime analysis. Returning a String from a
const function grants no retained DOM reference and no implicit subscription.
Explicit `Error!` contracts and ordinary recovery handle expected runtime
failures. Rejected or unsupported compile-time evaluation is not silently
treated as runtime work for a const function.

Future constrained directives may improve local contracts for required or
unique template contributions. Such constraints should check ordinary
semantics rather than introduce macros or hidden global routing. Their
source grammar must be accepted before examples teach it.

### When to use the pattern

Use reusable `HtmlPart` results for a navbar, sidebar, tabbed docs section
or pager that owns markup and resource dependencies. A straightforward
article can stay a direct top-level `[$md: ...]` fragment. Keep runtime
behaviour in a separately invoked function, explored in
@../browser-behaviour/ (Browser behaviour).

See @../../constants (Constants), @../../resources (Resources) and
@../../packages/builder/html/ (@html).
